window.NLP_CARDS = [{"n":0.1,"ti":"Text, Unicode & Normalisation","f":"p1-text-unicode-and-normalisation.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does len(\"café\") return in Python?","o":["4, the number of letters on screen","6, the number of UTF-8 bytes","5, the number of code points","1"],"a":2,"w":"c, a, f, e and a combining acute accent: five code points that display as four letters and take six bytes in UTF-8."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Text, Unicode & Normalisation","f":"p1-text-unicode-and-normalisation.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which normal form is lossless and the safe default for storing text?","o":["NFC","NFKC","NFKD","ASCII folding"],"a":0,"w":"NFC only merges canonically equivalent sequences; the K forms fold compatibility variants and change meaning (x² becomes x2)."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Text, Unicode & Normalisation","f":"p1-text-unicode-and-normalisation.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why use casefold() rather than lower() for case-insensitive matching?","o":["It is faster","It keeps accents","It handles emoji","It applies stronger mappings meant for comparison, such as ß to “ss”"],"a":3,"w":"“Straße”.lower() keeps ß; casefold() turns it into “ss”, so it matches “STRASSE”."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Text, Unicode & Normalisation","f":"p1-text-unicode-and-normalisation.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What did the uncased BERT tokeniser do to the Hindi word नमस्ते?","o":["Kept it whole","Stripped its vowel signs and virama, because they are combining marks, leaving a non-word","Turned it into [UNK]","Transliterated it to Latin letters"],"a":1,"w":"Accent stripping removes every combining mark, and in Devanagari those marks are vowels."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"Text, Unicode & Normalisation","f":"p1-text-unicode-and-normalisation.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why is a zero-width space a problem in a text pipeline?","o":["It doubles the file size","It is not valid UTF-8","It is invisible but breaks exact matching, filters and tokenisation","It is always removed by Python"],"a":2,"w":"“pass” + U+200B + “word” looks like password but is a different string; XLM-R split it into two words."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"PyTorch for Text: Batches, Padding & Masks","f":"p2-pytorch-for-text.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does an attention mask of 0 at a position mean in a Hugging Face batch?","o":["The token is important","The position is padding and should be ignored","The token is unknown","The sequence ends there and the next one starts"],"a":1,"w":"1 means a real token, 0 means padding. PyTorch’s src_key_padding_mask uses the opposite convention: True means ignore."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"PyTorch for Text: Batches, Padding & Masks","f":"p2-pytorch-for-text.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A model mean-pools token vectors without using the mask. What happens to its prediction for the same review as padding is added?","o":["Nothing","It becomes more confident","It raises an error","It drifts towards whatever the padding vector represents"],"a":3,"w":"Measured here: a 50-token positive review went from 70% to 51% positive when padded to 512 tokens."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"PyTorch for Text: Batches, Padding & Masks","f":"p2-pytorch-for-text.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does length bucketing do?","o":["Groups examples of similar length into the same batch so little padding is needed","Truncates long examples","Removes short examples","Sorts the whole dataset by length for every epoch"],"a":0,"w":"Sorting within chunks and shuffling the batches keeps most of the randomness while cutting padding."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"PyTorch for Text: Batches, Padding & Masks","f":"p2-pytorch-for-text.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why did padding each batch to its own maximum barely help on IMDB?","o":["Dynamic padding is broken in PyTorch","Reviews are all the same length","15% of reviews hit the 512 limit, so almost every random batch of 64 contains one and is padded to 512 anyway","The GPU ignores padding"],"a":2,"w":"With a long tail of lengths, only bucketing reduces padding much."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"PyTorch for Text: Batches, Padding & Masks","f":"p2-pytorch-for-text.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is the purpose of overfitting a single batch?","o":["To get the best test accuracy","To check the model and pipeline can learn at all before a long run","To tune the batch size","To measure GPU speed"],"a":1,"w":"A working model drives the loss on one batch to near zero; if it cannot, something is broken."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Regular Expressions & Text Cleaning","f":"p3-regex-and-text-cleaning.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why does \\S+@\\S+ often return “alice@example.com.” with a full stop?","o":["Python’s regex engine has a bug with full stops","The pattern is case-sensitive","\\S matches any non-space character, punctuation included","The full stop is part of the address"],"a":2,"w":"Measured on C4, 49% of its matches ended in punctuation. Restrict the characters allowed and end on a word boundary."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Regular Expressions & Text Cleaning","f":"p3-regex-and-text-cleaning.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What makes ^(a+)+$ slow on “aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaab”?","o":["There are exponentially many ways to split the a’s between the two quantifiers, and the engine tries them all before failing","The string is too long for the regex cache","Anchors are slow","The pattern is not compiled"],"a":0,"w":"Measured: 1.21 s for 26 a’s, about doubling per character; ^a+$ failed in under 0.005 ms."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Regular Expressions & Text Cleaning","f":"p3-regex-and-text-cleaning.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Removing scikit-learn’s English stop words cost an IMDB sentiment classifier 1.2 points. What is the main reason?","o":["The vocabulary became too small","Stop words carry the most sentiment","TF-IDF needs stop words to compute IDF","The list includes negations such as not, no and never, so “not good” becomes “good”"],"a":3,"w":"Keeping the ten negation words recovered about a third of the loss; other list words like but, too and very carry sentiment too."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Regular Expressions & Text Cleaning","f":"p3-regex-and-text-cleaning.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is the best tool for removing markup from real web pages?","o":["A regex such as <[^>]+>","An HTML parser, plus a boilerplate remover for menus and footers","Lower-casing","Unicode normalisation"],"a":1,"w":"A regex is fine for simple tags like <br />; scripts, comments, entities and boilerplate need a parser and content rules."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Regular Expressions & Text Cleaning","f":"p3-regex-and-text-cleaning.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A stricter date pattern found 312 dates on C4, 29% of them like 10/03/2015. What can a better regex do about those?","o":["Choose day-first, since it is more common","Choose month-first, since most pages are American","Nothing: the order is ambiguous in the text and needs metadata such as the page’s locale","Reject them all"],"a":2,"w":"Some ambiguity cannot be resolved by a pattern; record it rather than guessing silently."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Counting & Probability for Language","f":"p4-counting-and-probability.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"“The cat saw the dog” has how many tokens and types?","o":["5 tokens, 5 types","5 tokens, 4 types","4 tokens, 5 types","4 tokens, 4 types"],"a":1,"w":"Five occurrences, four distinct words: “the” appears twice."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Counting & Probability for Language","f":"p4-counting-and-probability.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does Zipf’s law say about word frequencies?","o":["All words are about equally frequent","Frequency grows with word length","Frequency is roughly proportional to 1/rank, so a few words are most of the text and there is a very long tail","Vocabulary size is fixed for each language"],"a":2,"w":"Measured on C4: the top 100 types cover 44% of tokens, and 46% of types appear exactly once."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Counting & Probability for Language","f":"p4-counting-and-probability.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why do language models add log-probabilities rather than multiply probabilities?","o":["The product of many small probabilities underflows to exactly zero in floating point","Logs make the probabilities larger","Multiplication is slower on GPUs","Probabilities cannot be multiplied"],"a":0,"w":"Measured: zero in float32 after 16 words, in float64 after 96."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Counting & Probability for Language","f":"p4-counting-and-probability.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"After “united”, “states” followed 64% of the time; after “the”, the most likely next word had about 1%. What does that say?","o":["“the” is a rare word","The bigram counts are wrong","“united” is more frequent than “the”","Some words constrain what follows much more than others, so the entropy of the next word depends on the context"],"a":3,"w":"Conditional distributions after function words are flat; after words in fixed phrases they are sharp."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Counting & Probability for Language","f":"p4-counting-and-probability.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A unigram model has an entropy of 10.93 bits per word. What is its perplexity?","o":["About 11","About 1,950 (2 10.93 )","About 110","It cannot be computed from the entropy"],"a":1,"w":"Perplexity is 2 to the power of the entropy in bits: an effective choice among about 1,950 equally likely words."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Sparse Vectors & Similarity","f":"p5-sparse-vectors-and-similarity.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"You repeat a document ten times. What happens to its dot product and its cosine with another document, using count vectors?","o":["Both stay the same","Both grow tenfold","The dot product grows tenfold; the cosine is unchanged","The cosine grows tenfold; the dot product is unchanged"],"a":2,"w":"Cosine divides by the vector lengths, which also grow tenfold, so only the direction counts."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Sparse Vectors & Similarity","f":"p5-sparse-vectors-and-similarity.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What do the three arrays of a CSR matrix store?","o":["The non-zero values, their column indices, and where each row starts","Rows, columns and the dense values","The values, their row indices and their column indices","A hash of each row"],"a":0,"w":"data, indices and indptr; row i is data[indptr[i]:indptr[i+1]]. Row and column for every entry is COO, not CSR."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Sparse Vectors & Similarity","f":"p5-sparse-vectors-and-similarity.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Ranking 2,000 IMDB reviews’ neighbours by raw dot products of word counts sent 52.6% of queries to one review. Which?","o":["The shortest review","A random review","A review with the same label","The longest review, which shares some words with everything"],"a":3,"w":"Unnormalised dot products reward length. Normalising rows (cosine) gave 1,792 distinct neighbours."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Sparse Vectors & Similarity","f":"p5-sparse-vectors-and-similarity.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Cosine on TF-IDF found that a Reese Witherspoon review’s nearest neighbours were other reviews of her films. What does that say about TF-IDF similarity?","o":["It measures sentiment directly","It is driven by shared rare words, so it finds the same topic","It is random","It only works for names"],"a":1,"w":"Rare words get the largest weights. Topic and sentiment line up only partly: the neighbour’s label matched 81% of the time."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Sparse Vectors & Similarity","f":"p5-sparse-vectors-and-similarity.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why was the dense float64 product 40 times slower than float32 on the GPU?","o":["float64 matrices cannot be multiplied on a GPU","The GPU ran out of memory","Consumer GPUs have far fewer 64-bit arithmetic units, and scipy’s default float64 was carried over silently","float64 has more zeros"],"a":2,"w":"Cast features to float32 before moving them to a GPU."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Linear Classifiers & Cross-Entropy","f":"p6-linear-classifiers.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A logistic regression gives a review a score of z = 0. What probability does it assign to the positive class?","o":["0%","50%","100%","It depends on the number of features"],"a":1,"w":"σ(0) = 1 / (1 + e⁰) = 0.5. Positive scores give more than 50%, negative less."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Linear Classifiers & Cross-Entropy","f":"p6-linear-classifiers.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"The model gives the correct class a probability of 1%. What is the cross-entropy loss for that example?","o":["0.01","0.99","0.69","About 4.6"],"a":3,"w":"−ln(0.01) ≈ 4.6. Confident mistakes are punished heavily; the loss has no upper limit."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Linear Classifiers & Cross-Entropy","f":"p6-linear-classifiers.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Past the best regularisation strength on IMDB, test accuracy barely changed but test log-loss rose from 0.24 to 0.37. What happened?","o":["The model became overconfident on the examples it gets wrong","The model got more examples wrong","The test set changed","Log-loss is unreliable"],"a":0,"w":"Accuracy only sees which side of 50% a prediction falls on; log-loss sees how sure it was."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Linear Classifiers & Cross-Entropy","f":"p6-linear-classifiers.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why did “7/10” never become a feature, while “10/10” did?","o":["It was too rare","It was removed as a stop word","scikit-learn’s default token pattern keeps only tokens of two or more characters, so “7” was dropped","The regulariser set its weight to zero"],"a":2,"w":"The tokeniser decides what the model can see. “10” has two characters and survived."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Linear Classifiers & Cross-Entropy","f":"p6-linear-classifiers.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"How many weight vectors does a softmax classifier with 4 classes have?","o":["One, shared by all classes","Four, one per class, each producing that class’s score","Sixteen, one per pair of classes","As many as there are training examples"],"a":1,"w":"Each class has its own weights and bias; softmax normalises the four scores into probabilities."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Metrics for NLP Tasks","f":"p7-metrics-for-nlp.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A model flags 100 tweets as offensive; 60 really are. There were 150 offensive tweets in total. What are its precision and recall?","o":["60% precision, 60% recall","40% precision, 60% recall","60% precision, 40% recall","40% precision, 40% recall"],"a":2,"w":"Precision = 60 / 100; recall = 60 / 150."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Metrics for NLP Tasks","f":"p7-metrics-for-nlp.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"For single-label multi-class classification, micro-averaged F1 equals what?","o":["Accuracy","Macro F1","The F1 of the largest class","Weighted F1"],"a":0,"w":"Pooling every decision, each error is one false positive for one class and one false negative for another, so micro P = micro R = accuracy."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Metrics for NLP Tasks","f":"p7-metrics-for-nlp.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"An NER model predicts “Union” as ORG where the gold entity is “European Union” (ORG). How does strict span scoring count it?","o":["As a true positive","As half a true positive","It is ignored","As one false positive and one false negative"],"a":3,"w":"The boundaries do not match, so the predicted span is wrong and the gold span is missed."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Metrics for NLP Tasks","f":"p7-metrics-for-nlp.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Two raters label a rare emotion. They agree 96% of the time, mostly by both saying “no”. Why is kappa better than raw agreement here?","o":["Kappa is always higher","Kappa subtracts the agreement expected by chance, which is very high when one answer dominates","Kappa counts only positive labels","Raw agreement cannot be computed for rare labels"],"a":1,"w":"Two raters who say “no” to everything agree almost always; kappa would be 0."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"Metrics for NLP Tasks","f":"p7-metrics-for-nlp.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Model B beats model A by 1.5 points of macro F1 on a 1,421-example test set. The bootstrap 95% interval of A’s macro F1 is six points wide. What can you conclude?","o":["B is better","A is better","Not much yet: compare them with a paired bootstrap on the same examples, or test on more data","The test set is wrong"],"a":2,"w":"Small differences on small test sets are often noise; a paired comparison is more sensitive than two separate intervals."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Datasets, Splits & Hugging Face","f":"p8-datasets-and-hugging-face.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Loading 127 MB of IMDB Arrow files changed the Python process’s memory by 0 MB. Why?","o":["The data was compressed","The files are memory-mapped: pages are read from disk only when accessed","The dataset was not actually loaded","Python frees memory immediately"],"a":1,"w":"Arrow files are mapped into memory by the operating system; reading the text into Python strings is what used 64 MB."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Datasets, Splits & Hugging Face","f":"p8-datasets-and-hugging-face.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"You rerun a map that took 2.5 seconds and it finishes in 0.05 seconds. What happened, and what is the risk?","o":["The GPU was used; there is no risk","The tokeniser warmed up; the risk is memory","Fewer examples were processed","The result was loaded from the cache by fingerprint; if you changed something the fingerprint cannot see, the result is stale"],"a":3,"w":"Use load_from_cache_file=False when a change might not be detected."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Datasets, Splits & Hugging Face","f":"p8-datasets-and-hugging-face.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why can a random split of movie reviews overstate accuracy, even with no duplicate texts?","o":["Reviews of the same film share names and plot words, so the model can recognise films seen in training","Random splits are always unbalanced","The test set becomes too small","Shuffling changes the labels"],"a":0,"w":"Split by film (as IMDB did), by author or by time, so test groups are new to the model."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Datasets, Splits & Hugging Face","f":"p8-datasets-and-hugging-face.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"When should you use streaming=True ?","o":["Always, because it is faster","When you need random access to examples","When the dataset is too large to download, or you only need part of it","When the dataset has no test split"],"a":2,"w":"Streaming reads records as you iterate and stores nothing; you lose indexing and exact global shuffling."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Datasets, Splits & Hugging Face","f":"p8-datasets-and-hugging-face.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A dataset card’s licence field says “unknown”. What should you do before shipping a model trained on it?","o":["Assume it is public domain","Find the original source and its terms, and get advice if they are unclear","Nothing; hub datasets are free to use","Remove the dataset card"],"a":1,"w":"The hub field is the uploader’s declaration; the source’s terms are what apply."},{"n":1,"ti":"What NLP Is","f":"01-what-nlp-is.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Finding every person, place and organisation name in a news article is which kind of task?","o":["One label per text","One label per token (sequence labelling)","Generating a new sequence","Scoring a pair of texts"],"a":1,"w":"Named-entity recognition tags each token, usually with B/I/O labels, and is scored on the resulting spans."},{"n":1,"ti":"What NLP Is","f":"01-what-nlp-is.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"“The trophy didn’t fit in the suitcase because it was too small.” What makes this hard for a word-counting model?","o":["It contains rare words","It is too long","Resolving “it” needs world knowledge and the whole sentence; swapping “small” for “big” changes the answer","It has no verbs"],"a":2,"w":"Reference resolution depends on meaning and context that word counts cannot capture."},{"n":1,"ti":"What NLP Is","f":"01-what-nlp-is.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why build a majority-class baseline?","o":["It shows what a model with no skill scores, so you can tell whether a real model has learned anything","It is often the best model","It is required by scikit-learn","It improves the other models"],"a":0,"w":"On SST-2 it scored about 51%; a method that scores 56% has learned very little."},{"n":1,"ti":"What NLP Is","f":"01-what-nlp-is.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What is the main practical advantage of a fine-tuned encoder over a zero-shot generative LLM for a high-volume classification task?","o":["It needs no labelled data","It can write explanations","It understands more languages","It is far smaller and faster per example"],"a":3,"w":"Measured here: DistilBERT classified about 9,200 sentences a second on one GPU; the 14.8-billion-parameter LLM about 5.6."},{"n":1,"ti":"What NLP Is","f":"01-what-nlp-is.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A word-list sentiment rule scored 56% on short sentences but higher on long reviews. Why?","o":["Long reviews are easier to understand","Long reviews contain more words, so the list finds some evidence more often instead of falling back to a default","The word list was trained on long reviews","Short sentences have no sentiment"],"a":1,"w":"Coverage is the first problem of rules: most short sentences contained none of the 50 words."},{"n":2,"ti":"Tokenisation, Stemming & Lemmas","f":"02-tokenisation-for-nlp.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"How does BPE build its vocabulary?","o":["By keeping the V most frequent words","By removing the least useful pieces from a large vocabulary","By repeatedly merging the most frequent adjacent pair of symbols, starting from characters","By looking words up in a dictionary"],"a":2,"w":"Pruning a large candidate set is Unigram; BPE grows the vocabulary one merge at a time."},{"n":2,"ti":"Tokenisation, Stemming & Lemmas","f":"02-tokenisation-for-nlp.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What is the main cost of a smaller subword vocabulary?","o":["Words are split into more pieces, so sequences get longer","More words become unknown","The model cannot handle punctuation","Training the tokeniser takes longer"],"a":0,"w":"Measured: BPE averaged 1.53 pieces per review word with 1,000 pieces and 1.03 with 32,000."},{"n":2,"ti":"Tokenisation, Stemming & Lemmas","f":"02-tokenisation-for-nlp.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does a lemmatiser need that a stemmer does not?","o":["A GPU","A list of suffixes","A subword vocabulary","A dictionary of word forms and the word’s part of speech"],"a":3,"w":"“better” is lemmatised to “good” only when tagged as an adjective; a stemmer just strips suffixes by rule."},{"n":2,"ti":"Tokenisation, Stemming & Lemmas","f":"02-tokenisation-for-nlp.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A word-level vocabulary built from film reviews is used on news headlines. What happens to the unknown-word rate?","o":["It falls, because news uses simpler words","It rises, because the new domain has words and names the reviews never used","It stays the same","It drops to zero"],"a":1,"w":"Measured: 3.2% of review words but 13.6% of news words fell outside a 16,000-word review vocabulary."},{"n":2,"ti":"Tokenisation, Stemming & Lemmas","f":"02-tokenisation-for-nlp.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why does WordPiece mark pieces with “##”?","o":["To show they are rare","To mark unknown words","To mark that a piece continues the previous one, so words can be reassembled","To separate sentences"],"a":2,"w":"“unwatchable” might become “un ##watch ##able”: the marks tell you where word boundaries were."},{"n":3,"ti":"Bag of Words & TF-IDF","f":"03-bag-of-words-and-tf-idf.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A word appears in every training document. What is its IDF under scikit-learn’s smoothed formula?","o":["0","1, the minimum: ln((1 + N) / (1 + N)) + 1","N","It is removed automatically"],"a":1,"w":"The +1 keeps universal words in the vector with the smallest possible weight rather than zeroing them."},{"n":3,"ti":"Bag of Words & TF-IDF","f":"03-bag-of-words-and-tf-idf.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does sublinear tf (1 + ln count) change?","o":["It removes rare words","It adds bigrams","It makes the vector dense","Repeated occurrences of a word add less and less weight"],"a":3,"w":"Ten mentions of “great” count about 3.3 times one mention, not 10 times."},{"n":3,"ti":"Bag of Words & TF-IDF","f":"03-bag-of-words-and-tf-idf.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why fit the TF-IDF vectoriser on the training split only?","o":["Otherwise the vocabulary and IDF values are computed using the test data, which leaks information","It is faster","The test split has no labels","scikit-learn requires it"],"a":0,"w":"Fit on train, then transform both train and test with the same fitted object."},{"n":3,"ti":"Bag of Words & TF-IDF","f":"03-bag-of-words-and-tf-idf.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What is the main drawback of the hashing trick?","o":["It needs a GPU","It cannot handle new words","Different n-grams can collide in one column, and columns cannot be mapped back to words","It only works for binary features"],"a":2,"w":"Measured: 2^18 columns lost 0.3–0.4 points; 2^10 columns lost 13–18 points to collisions."},{"n":3,"ti":"Bag of Words & TF-IDF","f":"03-bag-of-words-and-tf-idf.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Removing stop words hurt sentiment classification but not topic classification here. Why the difference?","o":["Topic datasets have no stop words","Sentiment depends on words like not, but and very that are on stop lists; topic depends on content words","Stop words are only removed from short texts","The topic model used bigrams"],"a":1,"w":"Measured: removing scikit-learn’s stop words cost 1.5 points on IMDB and 0.1 on AG News."},{"n":4,"ti":"Text Classification with Linear Models","f":"04-text-classification.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What is the “naive” assumption in naive Bayes?","o":["All classes are equally likely","Word order matters","Words are independent of each other given the class","Every document has the same length"],"a":2,"w":"It lets P(document | class) be a product of per-word probabilities, a sum in log space."},{"n":4,"ti":"Text Classification with Linear Models","f":"04-text-classification.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why does naive Bayes use Laplace (add-α) smoothing?","o":["So a word never seen with a class does not make that class’s probability zero","To make training faster","To remove stop words","To calibrate the probabilities"],"a":0,"w":"Without it, one unseen word multiplies the product by zero (P4)."},{"n":4,"ti":"Text Classification with Linear Models","f":"04-text-classification.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"According to the generative–discriminative trade-off, which is usually true?","o":["Logistic regression is always better","Naive Bayes is always better","They are identical","Naive Bayes approaches its best accuracy with less data; logistic regression has a higher best with enough data"],"a":3,"w":"That is the theory (Ng and Jordan, 2001). In this chapter’s IMDB measurements logistic regression still led at every size, so treat it as something to test."},{"n":4,"ti":"Text Classification with Linear Models","f":"04-text-classification.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Naive Bayes gives 99.9% confidence to most of its predictions. What does that usually mean?","o":["It is almost always right","It is overconfident: summing many correlated word log-probabilities pushes outputs to the extremes","The data is too easy","Smoothing was too strong"],"a":1,"w":"Measured: 92.7% of NB’s IMDB predictions were above 99% confidence, and it was right on 87.8% of those."},{"n":4,"ti":"Text Classification with Linear Models","f":"04-text-classification.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A classifier trained on 90% negatives misses most positives. Which change does not address that?","o":["Class weights in training","Lowering the decision threshold","Reporting accuracy instead of recall","Collecting more positive examples"],"a":2,"w":"Accuracy hides the problem; the other three each attack it."},{"n":5,"ti":"N-gram Language Models & Smoothing","f":"05-n-gram-language-models.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does the Markov assumption in a trigram model say?","o":["Every word is independent","Each word depends only on the two words before it","Each word depends on the whole sentence","The model must be trained with gradient descent"],"a":1,"w":"P(w t | w 1 … w t−1 ) ≈ P(w t | w t−2 w t−1 )."},{"n":5,"ti":"N-gram Language Models & Smoothing","f":"05-n-gram-language-models.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A model has a perplexity of 200 on test text. What does that mean?","o":["It gets 200 words right","Its vocabulary has 200 words","It is 200 times better than chance","On average it is as uncertain as choosing uniformly among 200 words"],"a":3,"w":"PPL = exp(average negative log-probability per word): an effective branching factor."},{"n":5,"ti":"N-gram Language Models & Smoothing","f":"05-n-gram-language-models.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why is the unsmoothed (maximum-likelihood) trigram model’s test perplexity infinite?","o":["Some test trigrams never occurred in training, so they get probability zero","The vocabulary is too large","Trigrams are too short","Perplexity is always infinite for trigrams"],"a":0,"w":"Measured: 59.1% of test trigrams never occurred in the 2.1 million training words."},{"n":5,"ti":"N-gram Language Models & Smoothing","f":"05-n-gram-language-models.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What is Kneser–Ney’s continuation count for a word?","o":["How many times the word occurs","How long the word is","How many different words it follows","How many sentences it ends"],"a":2,"w":"“Francisco” is frequent but follows almost only “San”, so its continuation count is small."},{"n":5,"ti":"N-gram Language Models & Smoothing","f":"05-n-gram-language-models.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why can you not report perplexity for Google’s “stupid backoff”?","o":["It is too slow","Its scores do not form a probability distribution","It uses neural networks","It only works for bigrams"],"a":1,"w":"It multiplies by a fixed 0.4 at each backoff step without renormalising."},{"n":6,"ti":"Word Embeddings: word2vec from Scratch","f":"06-word2vec-from-scratch.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"In skip-gram, what predicts what?","o":["The surrounding words predict the centre word","The centre word predicts each word in its window","Each word predicts the next sentence","The document predicts its label"],"a":1,"w":"CBOW is the reverse: the averaged context predicts the centre."},{"n":6,"ti":"Word Embeddings: word2vec from Scratch","f":"06-word2vec-from-scratch.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does negative sampling replace?","o":["The embedding table","The context window","Subsampling","A softmax over the whole vocabulary, with a binary task against a few sampled noise words"],"a":3,"w":"Each update touches only the centre, the context and k noise rows."},{"n":6,"ti":"Word Embeddings: word2vec from Scratch","f":"06-word2vec-from-scratch.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why are “quickly” and “slowly” nearest neighbours in word2vec?","o":["Antonyms appear in almost the same contexts, and word2vec learns from contexts","They share letters","The model was trained too briefly","Cosine similarity ignores meaning"],"a":0,"w":"“Moved quickly” and “moved slowly”: same neighbours, opposite meaning."},{"n":6,"ti":"Word Embeddings: word2vec from Scratch","f":"06-word2vec-from-scratch.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Which change helped analogy accuracy most in the measurements?","o":["300 dimensions instead of 100","15 negatives instead of 5","Three passes over the corpus instead of one","A window of 2 instead of 5"],"a":2,"w":"37.6% → 50.1%. 300 dimensions gave 38.1%; 15 negatives 40.5%; a window of 2 lowered it to 28.0%."},{"n":6,"ti":"Word Embeddings: word2vec from Scratch","f":"06-word2vec-from-scratch.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"How is the analogy “a : b :: c : ?” answered with word vectors?","o":["By finding the word most similar to c","By finding the word nearest to b − a + c, excluding a, b and c","By averaging a, b and c","By training a classifier on analogies"],"a":1,"w":"king − man + woman lands nearest to queen."},{"n":7,"ti":"GloVe, fastText & Evaluating Embeddings","f":"07-glove-fasttext-and-evaluation.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does GloVe’s training objective fit?","o":["The probability of the next word","A label for each word","Dot products of word vectors to the log of their co-occurrence counts, weighted by f(X)","Character n-grams"],"a":2,"w":"J = Σ f(X ij )(w i ·w̃ j + b i + b̃ j − log X ij )², over non-zero pairs."},{"n":7,"ti":"GloVe, fastText & Evaluating Embeddings","f":"07-glove-fasttext-and-evaluation.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"How does fastText produce a vector for a word it never saw in training?","o":["By summing the vectors of its character n-grams","It uses the vector of [UNK]","It asks a dictionary","It cannot"],"a":0,"w":"“witherspooon” shares most of its n-grams with “witherspoon”, so their vectors are close."},{"n":7,"ti":"GloVe, fastText & Evaluating Embeddings","f":"07-glove-fasttext-and-evaluation.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why do distributional embeddings score low on SimLex-999?","o":["SimLex is too small","SimLex uses rare words","The embeddings are too short","SimLex rates similarity, not relatedness, and antonyms that share contexts are rated very dissimilar"],"a":3,"w":"“coffee” and “cup” are related but not similar; “hot” and “cold” share contexts but are opposites."},{"n":7,"ti":"GloVe, fastText & Evaluating Embeddings","f":"07-glove-fasttext-and-evaluation.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Which embedding should you use in your classifier?","o":["The one with the highest analogy accuracy","The one that gives the best validation score on your own task","Always the largest pretrained one","Always fastText"],"a":1,"w":"Measured: the analogy ranking and the SST-2 ranking disagreed."},{"n":7,"ti":"GloVe, fastText & Evaluating Embeddings","f":"07-glove-fasttext-and-evaluation.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Projecting occupations onto she − he in pretrained GloVe put “nurse” at +0.28 and “engineer” at −0.16. What does that show?","o":["GloVe has a bug","The words are misspelt","The vectors absorbed gendered associations present in their training text","Nothing; projections are random"],"a":2,"w":"Embeddings reflect the statistics of the corpus, social patterns included."},{"n":8,"ti":"Neural Text Classifiers","f":"08-neural-text-classifiers.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does max-over-time pooling in a text CNN keep?","o":["The average score of each filter","Each filter’s highest score anywhere in the text","The position of the most important word","The last token’s vector"],"a":1,"w":"The model learns whether a phrase occurred, not where."},{"n":8,"ti":"Neural Text Classifiers","f":"08-neural-text-classifiers.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does an embedding-bag classifier lose compared with a CNN?","o":["The ability to use pretrained vectors","Speed","Word order, even locally: “not good” and “good, not” look the same","The ability to train on a GPU"],"a":2,"w":"Averaging is order-free; hashed bigrams (fastText) put a little local order back."},{"n":8,"ti":"Neural Text Classifiers","f":"08-neural-text-classifiers.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Frozen GloVe embeddings in an embedding bag scored far below fine-tuned ones on SST-2. Why?","o":["GloVe places sentiment opposites like “good” and “bad” close together, and frozen vectors cannot move them apart","GloVe has no vectors for common words","Frozen embeddings cannot be averaged","Fine-tuning uses a different dataset"],"a":0,"w":"Measured: 73.47% frozen against 81.35% fine-tuned on SST-2."},{"n":8,"ti":"Neural Text Classifiers","f":"08-neural-text-classifiers.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why train each model with several seeds?","o":["To use more GPU","Because each seed sees different data","To get a bigger model","Because random initialisation and batch order alone change the result, and one run can mislead"],"a":3,"w":"Measured: the fastText-style model’s three SST-2 runs ranged from 77.4% to 80.5%."},{"n":8,"ti":"Neural Text Classifiers","f":"08-neural-text-classifiers.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"When is TF-IDF with logistic regression most likely to beat a small neural model?","o":["When there are millions of training examples","When labelled data is limited and the signal is in specific words and phrases","When the texts are in many languages","Never"],"a":1,"w":"Measured on IMDB: TF-IDF led the best neural model by 4.5 points at 1,000 reviews and still by 2.8 at 25,000."},{"n":9,"ti":"Recurrent Networks & LSTMs from Scratch","f":"09-rnns-and-lstms.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"In an LSTM, what makes gradients survive many steps?","o":["The tanh on the output","Larger hidden states","The additive cell update, whose derivative along the cell path is the forget gate, which can stay near 1","Dropout"],"a":2,"w":"c t = f ⊙ c t−1 + i ⊙ g: no squashing nonlinearity on the path back through the cell."},{"n":9,"ti":"Recurrent Networks & LSTMs from Scratch","f":"09-rnns-and-lstms.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does truncated BPTT with a window of 35 tokens prevent?","o":["Learning dependencies longer than 35 tokens through gradients, although the state still carries information forward","Using any information older than 35 tokens","Exploding gradients entirely","Training on long documents"],"a":0,"w":"Measured: with a 5-token window the LSTM language model reached 163.7 against 131.1 with 35 tokens."},{"n":9,"ti":"Recurrent Networks & LSTMs from Scratch","f":"09-rnns-and-lstms.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A vanilla RNN’s per-step gradient factor is 0.9. Roughly how much gradient reaches a token 50 steps back?","o":["90%","45%","10%","About 0.5% (0.9 50 )"],"a":3,"w":"0.9 50 ≈ 0.005. Exponential decay is why plain RNNs forget."},{"n":9,"ti":"Recurrent Networks & LSTMs from Scratch","f":"09-rnns-and-lstms.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why is a bidirectional RNN not used to generate text?","o":["It is too slow","Its backward direction reads the future, which does not exist yet when generating","It has no hidden state","It cannot be trained with BPTT"],"a":1,"w":"For classification and tagging the whole input is available, so both directions help."},{"n":9,"ti":"Recurrent Networks & LSTMs from Scratch","f":"09-rnns-and-lstms.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why are RNNs slow to train on a GPU compared with CNNs and transformers?","o":["They have more parameters","They need more data","Each step depends on the previous one, so the time steps cannot be computed in parallel","They use sigmoid functions"],"a":2,"w":"Measured, from the other side: the 5-token BPTT run took 2.4× as long as the 35-token one, because it ran seven times as many sequential steps."},{"n":10,"ti":"Sequence Labelling: POS Tagging & NER","f":"10-sequence-labelling.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"In BIO tagging, how is “New York City” as a location tagged?","o":["LOC LOC LOC","B-LOC I-LOC I-LOC","B-LOC E-LOC E-LOC","S-LOC S-LOC S-LOC"],"a":1,"w":"B begins the entity, I continues it. In BIOES it would be B-LOC I-LOC E-LOC."},{"n":10,"ti":"Sequence Labelling: POS Tagging & NER","f":"10-sequence-labelling.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Which BIO sequence is invalid?","o":["B-PER I-PER O","B-PER B-PER O","O B-LOC O","O I-PER I-PER"],"a":3,"w":"I-PER must follow B-PER or I-PER. A softmax tagger can still output it; the BiLSTM tagger here produced 168 · 136 · 134 of them in three runs."},{"n":10,"ti":"Sequence Labelling: POS Tagging & NER","f":"10-sequence-labelling.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why add a word-shape feature to a tagger that uses lower-cased GloVe embeddings?","o":["Lower-casing removes capitalisation, which is a strong clue that a word is a name","To make the vocabulary smaller","GloVe has no vectors for capital letters","To speed up training"],"a":0,"w":"“Apple” and “apple”, “US” and “us” look identical after lower-casing."},{"n":10,"ti":"Sequence Labelling: POS Tagging & NER","f":"10-sequence-labelling.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does BIOES add over BIO?","o":["More entity types","A tag for punctuation","Explicit end (E) and single-token (S) tags, so every entity’s end is marked","Probabilities for each tag"],"a":2,"w":"Measured with a softmax BiLSTM, BIOES scored 83.47 against BIO’s 84.07: the extra tags did not help without constraints."},{"n":10,"ti":"Sequence Labelling: POS Tagging & NER","f":"10-sequence-labelling.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A POS tagger is 95% accurate overall. Why report its accuracy on unknown words separately?","o":["Unknown words are always tagged correctly","Most test words were seen in training, so the overall number hides how the tagger copes with new words, which is where it will fail on new text","Unknown words have no tags","It is required by the CoNLL format"],"a":1,"w":"Measured: 12.18% of test tokens were unseen; the baseline got 46.07% of them right and the BiLSTM 78.43%."},{"n":11,"ti":"CRFs & Structured Prediction","f":"11-crfs-and-viterbi.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"How many tag sequences does Viterbi effectively consider for 10 tokens and 9 tags, and how many operations does it need?","o":["90 sequences, 90 operations","9 10 sequences, 9 10 operations","9 10 sequences, about 10 × 9² operations","10 9 sequences, 10 operations"],"a":2,"w":"Dynamic programming keeps only the best path into each tag at each step."},{"n":11,"ti":"CRFs & Structured Prediction","f":"11-crfs-and-viterbi.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"What can a CRF use that an HMM cannot?","o":["Arbitrary, overlapping features of the whole input, such as capitalisation or the next word, for every tag decision","Transition probabilities","Dynamic programming","Labelled training data"],"a":0,"w":"An HMM generates each word from its tag alone; a CRF conditions on the observed input."},{"n":11,"ti":"CRFs & Structured Prediction","f":"11-crfs-and-viterbi.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"What does the forward algorithm compute in CRF training?","o":["The single best tag sequence","The gradient of the BiLSTM","The emission scores","The log of the sum of scores over all possible tag sequences, the normaliser of P(tags | words)"],"a":3,"w":"It is Viterbi with max replaced by logsumexp."},{"n":11,"ti":"CRFs & Structured Prediction","f":"11-crfs-and-viterbi.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"After training, the CRF’s transition score from O to I-PER is strongly negative. What did it learn?","o":["That O is rare","That an entity cannot continue without having begun, a BIO constraint it found in the data","That PER entities are short","Nothing; transitions are random"],"a":1,"w":"Measured: O → I-PER -1.11, B-PER → I-PER 0.96."},{"n":11,"ti":"CRFs & Structured Prediction","f":"11-crfs-and-viterbi.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Viterbi beat greedy decoding by under one point on an HMM POS tagger, yet changed tags in over 40% of sentences. Why is the average gain so small?","o":["Viterbi was implemented wrongly","POS tags do not depend on each other","With strong emissions, the locally best tag is usually also on the globally best path; Viterbi changes the few sentences where context overturns a local choice","Greedy decoding is a form of Viterbi"],"a":2,"w":"Measured: 88.03% against 87.16%, with 1,487 of 3,453 sentences changed."},{"n":12,"ti":"Seq2Seq: Encoder–Decoder RNNs","f":"12-seq2seq-encoder-decoder.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"In a seq2seq model without attention, what does the decoder know about the source sentence?","o":["Every source word’s vector","Only the single context vector the encoder produced","The source sentence’s length","Nothing"],"a":1,"w":"All source information passes through one fixed-size vector: the bottleneck."},{"n":12,"ti":"Seq2Seq: Encoder–Decoder RNNs","f":"12-seq2seq-encoder-decoder.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"What is teacher forcing?","o":["Feeding the decoder the gold previous token during training, instead of its own prediction","Training the encoder before the decoder","Using a larger model to supervise a smaller one","Forcing the output to a fixed length"],"a":0,"w":"Measured: with no teacher forcing the model reached 15.13 BLEU against 21.84 with it."},{"n":12,"ti":"Seq2Seq: Encoder–Decoder RNNs","f":"12-seq2seq-encoder-decoder.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why does beam search need length normalisation?","o":["To make it faster","To keep the beam a fixed size","To avoid repeated words","Each extra token multiplies in a probability below one, so unnormalised scores favour short outputs"],"a":3,"w":"Dividing the log-probability by length α removes most of that bias."},{"n":12,"ti":"Seq2Seq: Encoder–Decoder RNNs","f":"12-seq2seq-encoder-decoder.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"What is beam search with a beam width of 1?","o":["Random sampling","Exhaustive search","Greedy decoding","Viterbi"],"a":2,"w":"It keeps only the single best partial sequence at each step."},{"n":12,"ti":"Seq2Seq: Encoder–Decoder RNNs","f":"12-seq2seq-encoder-decoder.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why did translation quality fall on longer sentences without attention?","o":["Long sentences have rarer words","A fixed-size context vector cannot hold all the information of a long sentence","The decoder has a maximum length","BLEU penalises long outputs"],"a":1,"w":"Measured without attention: 24.5 BLEU at 10–15 words, 11.58 at 20+."},{"n":13,"ti":"Attention for Seq2Seq","f":"13-attention-for-seq2seq.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"With attention, what does the decoder read at each step?","o":["Only the last encoder state","The average of all encoder states, the same at every step","A weighted average of all encoder states, with weights recomputed for that step","The previous target word only"],"a":2,"w":"c t = Σ α ti h i , with new α at every output position."},{"n":13,"ti":"Attention for Seq2Seq","f":"13-attention-for-seq2seq.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"How is the multiplicative (Luong “general”) score computed?","o":["s · W h i , a bilinear product of decoder and encoder states","v · tanh(W h i + U s)","‖s − h i ‖","By counting shared words"],"a":0,"w":"The additive form is Bahdanau’s; the dot-product family is what transformers use."},{"n":13,"ti":"Attention for Seq2Seq","f":"13-attention-for-seq2seq.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Where did attention help most in the measurements?","o":["Short sentences","Sentences with rare words","It helped equally everywhere","Long sentences, where the single context vector is most overloaded"],"a":3,"w":"Measured gains from additive attention: +10.0 BLEU under 10 words, +16.5 at 15–19 words, and on 20-plus-word sentences 11.58 → 24.92, more than double."},{"n":13,"ti":"Attention for Seq2Seq","f":"13-attention-for-seq2seq.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why does a sharper query (larger dot products) concentrate the attention weights?","o":["It changes the keys","Softmax of larger score differences puts more weight on the largest score","It removes padding","It normalises the context vector"],"a":1,"w":"This is also why transformers scale scores by 1/√d: without it, large vectors make the softmax nearly one-hot."},{"n":13,"ti":"Attention for Seq2Seq","f":"13-attention-for-seq2seq.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"What did the transformer change about attention-based seq2seq?","o":["It removed attention","It added a second bottleneck vector","It used self-attention within each sequence, several heads, and no recurrence, so all positions are computed in parallel","It used characters instead of words"],"a":2,"w":"Chapter 15 builds one."},{"n":14,"ti":"Machine Translation & BLEU","f":"14-machine-translation-and-bleu.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"What does “clipping” in BLEU’s n-gram precision prevent?","o":["Long outputs","Credit for repeating a word more times than it appears in the reference","Matching punctuation","Using more than one reference"],"a":1,"w":"“the the the the” against “the cat” gets 1 of 4 unigrams, not 4 of 4."},{"n":14,"ti":"Machine Translation & BLEU","f":"14-machine-translation-and-bleu.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"What is the brevity penalty for?","o":["Penalising long outputs","Speeding up scoring","Penalising rare words","Stopping a system from scoring high precision by writing only a few safe words"],"a":3,"w":"Without it, a system writing only its few most confident words would score high precision. Here the attention model’s outputs were slightly short, giving a penalty of 0.965."},{"n":14,"ti":"Machine Translation & BLEU","f":"14-machine-translation-and-bleu.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Inserting “not” after the first “is” or “are” of each reference barely lowered BLEU. What does that show?","o":["BLEU counts overlapping n-grams, so a meaning-reversing change of one word costs little","BLEU understands negation","The references were wrong","Negation is rare in captions"],"a":0,"w":"Measured: with “not” inserted into 366 of the 1,000 references, BLEU was 91.8, the same as for synonym swaps that keep the meaning (91.4)."},{"n":14,"ti":"Machine Translation & BLEU","f":"14-machine-translation-and-bleu.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"Why does chrF give partial credit for “bikes” against “bike”?","o":["It uses a dictionary","It lower-cases everything","It matches character n-grams, and the two words share most of theirs","It ignores the last letter"],"a":2,"w":"That makes chrF fairer for languages with rich inflection."},{"n":14,"ti":"Machine Translation & BLEU","f":"14-machine-translation-and-bleu.html","lv":"Intermediate","q":"What does a learned metric like COMET use that BLEU does not?","o":["More references","A neural model trained on human quality ratings, which can recognise meaning-preserving paraphrases","Longer n-grams","The training data of the system"],"a":1,"w":"It is also slower and less transparent; neither replaces human checks for important decisions."}];
